  "narrative": "I interpreted each returned interval according to the signed-to-direction convention: a positive signed estimate supports the registered claim direction, whereas a negative signed estimate contradicts it. The effect-size threshold is 0.15 composite dependency-score units. H1-H3 and H7 are very close to zero and far below threshold; these claims are rejected and closed. H6 is the clearest discovery-supported association: KEAP1 loss has signed mean difference 0.810 with 95% interval 0.745 to 0.875, meaning KEAP1-loss cells have a higher composite dependency score, or weaker measured dependency, than comparator cells. The effect is very large on the outcome scale and the interval is entirely above the threshold, so I accept it as a strong association in this supplied dataset and request independent validation. H4 is directionally refuted as written: the signed interval is entirely negative, but its magnitude is large. This indicates a robust opposite raw association—TP53 loss is associated with a higher composite score/weak dependency—not the originally claimed stronger dependency. I therefore reject the original directional claim but keep the line active and register a reversed TP53 claim for formal testing. H5 and the D/E/F marker interactions are not meaningful. H10 is not supported in the claimed direction. H11 has a point estimate of 0.127, below threshold, with an interval spanning the null and reaching above threshold; it is ambiguous and remains unresolved rather than accepted or definitively rejected. H12 is not supported in its original direction; although its point estimate exceeds the threshold in magnitude, it is opposite to the registered direction, interval includes null, and subgroup cell sizes are modest, so I do not pursue it now. All conclusions are dataset-specific associations, not causal or biological mechanism claims.",
  "research_notes": "Discovery evidence so far: KEAP1 loss is accepted in this supplied dataset as a strong positive association with dependency_composite (weaker dependency), signed effect ~0.81, interval well above threshold; independent validation requested for H6. TP53 loss has a large opposite-to-registered-direction effect; original H4 rejected, reversed TP53 proposal registered and active. KRAS main effect, media-serum contrast, constructed-marker main effects, and D/E/F interactions are null or closed. KEAP1-by-KRAS interaction is borderline and unresolved, not accepted. MYC-by-TP53 interaction is directionally inconsistent with original claim and uncertain; closed. Next priorities: await validation of H6; if supported, examine robustness/technical confounding for KEAP1 and TP53 using available technical/culture covariates only as sensitivity checks. Avoid biological interpretation of constructed markers or composite score beyond association in this dataset.",
